#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# Symmetries and differential algebra

#% text
# Lie point symmetries of differential equations and what they give: canonical coordinates, reduction of order, solutions, similarity reductions of partial differential equations, conservation laws. Then differential algebra: rankings and reduction of differential polynomials, the Rosenfeld-Groebner and Thomas decompositions of systems, and the Painleve test.

#% section
# Lie point symmetries

#% text
# A generator `xi d/dx + eta d/dy` is written as the list `[xi, eta]`. `lie_point_symmetries` sets up the determining equations, solves them, and gives a basis of the symmetry algebra.

#% cell
lie_point_symmetries(y''(x) == y(x)^(-3), y, x)
#% output
#= [[2*x, y], [x^2, x*y], [1, 0]]

#% text
# The determining equations themselves are linear partial differential equations for the infinitesimals:

#% cell
symmetry_determining_equations(y''(x) == 0, y, x)
#% output
#= [-derivative(0, 2)(xi)(x, y) == 0, -2*derivative(1, 1)(xi)(x, y) + derivative(0, 2)(eta)(x, y) == 0, -derivative(2, 0)(xi)(x, y) + 2*derivative(1, 1)(eta)(x, y) == 0, derivative(2, 0)(eta)(x, y) == 0]

#% text
# The commutators of the generators, in the basis `v(1), v(2), ...`: here the algebra sl(2).

#% cell
lie_commutator_table([[2*x, y], [x^2, x*y], [1, 0]], [x, y])
#% output
#= [[0, 2*v(2), -2*v(3)], [-2*v(2), 0, -v(1)], [2*v(3), v(1), 0]]

#% text
# For partial differential equations a generator lists the coefficients of all independent variables, then of the function: the Korteweg-de Vries equation has translations, a Galilean boost and a scaling.

#% cell
lie_point_symmetries(derivative(0, 1)(u)(x, t) + u(x, t)*derivative(1, 0)(u)(x, t) + derivative(3, 0)(u)(x, t) == 0, u, [x, t])
#% output
#= [[0, 1, 0], [(1/3)*x, t, (-2/3)*u], [1, 0, 0], [t, 0, 1]]

#% section
# Using a symmetry

#% text
# In canonical coordinates `r, s` a symmetry becomes a translation in `s`, so the equation drops one order.

#% cell
canonical_coordinates([x, y], [x, y])
#% output
#= [y/x, log(x)]

#% cell
symmetry_reduce_order(y''(x) == y(x)^(-3), y, x, [1, 0], v, r)
#% output
#= [-v(r)^3 - r^3*v'(r) == 0, r == y, v == y'(x)^-1]

#% text
# `symmetry_d_solve` carries the reduction through to the solutions:

#% cell
symmetry_d_solve(y'(x) == (y(x)/x)^2 + y(x)/x, y, x)
#% output
#= [[y(x) -> x/(c(1) - log(x))]]

#% text
# For a partial differential equation, the solutions invariant under a symmetry satisfy an ordinary differential equation: the scaling of the heat equation gives the similarity variable `x/sqrt(t)`.

#% cell
similarity_reduction(derivative(0, 1)(u)(x, t) == derivative(2, 0)(u)(x, t), u, [x, t], [x, 2*t, 0], f, z)
#% output
#= [-2*f''(z) - z*f'(z) == 0, u(x, t) -> f(x/t^(1/2)), z -> x/t^(1/2)]

#% section
# Conservation laws

#% text
# A multiplier `Lambda` makes `Lambda eq` a divergence. For an evolution equation `conservation_laws` gives the multiplier, the density and the flux:

#% cell
conservation_laws(derivative(0, 1)(u)(x, t) + u(x, t)*derivative(1, 0)(u)(x, t) + derivative(3, 0)(u)(x, t) == 0, u, [x, t])[3]
#% output
#= [1, u(x, t), (1/2)*u(x, t)^2 + derivative(2, 0)(u)(x, t)]

#% text
# For an ordinary differential equation the laws are first integrals:

#% cell
conservation_laws(y''(x) + y(x)^3 == 0, y, x, order: 1, degree: 1)
#% output
#= [[y'(x), (1/4)*y(x)^4 + (1/2)*y'(x)^2]]

#% section
# Differential polynomials

#% text
# A ranking orders the derivatives: `orderly` (by total order first, the default) or `elimination` (by function first). The leader, initial and separant of a differential polynomial follow from it.

#% cell
[differential_leader(y'(x)^2 - 4*y(x), y, x), differential_initial(x*y(x)*y'(x)^2 + y(x)^3, y, x), differential_separant(y'(x)^2 - 4*y(x), y, x)]
#% output
#= [y'(x), x*y(x), 2*y'(x)]

#% text
# Reduction eliminates derivatives of the leaders; a member of the differential ideal reduces to 0.

#% cell
differential_reduce(d(y'(x)^2 - 4*y(x), x, x), [y'(x)^2 - 4*y(x)], y, x)
#% output
#= 0

#% section
# Decompositions of systems

#% text
# `rosenfeld_groebner` splits the solutions into regular differential chains with inequations: `y'^2 == 4 y` has the general solutions and the singular solution `y == 0`.

#% cell
rosenfeld_groebner(y'(x)^2 - 4*y(x), y, x)
#% output
#= [[[-4*y(x) + y'(x)^2 == 0], [y'(x) != 0]], [[y(x) == 0], []]]

#% text
# An elimination ranking separates the functions:

#% cell
rosenfeld_groebner([y'(x) - z(x), z'(x) + y(x)], [y, z], x, ranking: elimination)
#% output
#= [[[z(x) + z''(x) == 0, y(x) + z'(x) == 0], []]]

#% text
# `thomas_decomposition` gives disjoint simple systems (for ordinary differential equations):

#% cell
thomas_decomposition(y(x)*y''(x) - y'(x)^2, y, x)
#% output
#= [[[-y'(x)^2 + y(x)*y''(x) == 0], [y(x) != 0]], [[y(x) == 0], []]]

#% section
# The Painleve test

#% text
# The test finds the leading behaviour at a movable singularity, the resonances and the compatibility conditions:

#% cell
painleve_test(y''(x) == 6*y(x)^2 + a*x^2, y, x)
#% output
#= [[leading_exponent -> -2, leading_coefficient -> 1, resonances -> [-1, 6], conditions -> [-a], passes -> false]]

#% section
# Common mistakes

#% item
# Derivatives of functions of several variables are written `derivative(i, j)(u)(x, t)`; `u_t` or `d(u(x, t), t)` inside a call to these functions are not recognised as derivatives of `u` until evaluated.

#% item
# Generators are lists over the variables in the order independent, then dependent: `[xi, tau, eta]` for `u(x, t)` with `[x, t]`.

#% item
# First-order ordinary differential equations have infinitely many symmetries; `lie_point_symmetries` then returns the general form with arbitrary functions. Use `degree: k` for polynomial symmetries.

#% section
# Related functions

